ACCOUNTING documentation, last updated on 23 Sep 2026

"Accounting" is what the server knows about who a user is: which
account it is logged in to, and what host it is shown under.  The two
halves come from opposite directions, and that is the whole shape of
this file:

  - The host is this server's own.  Every user's host is hidden from
    the moment it is registered, as a cipher of its real address under
    a key every server shares.  There is no opting out, for the user or
    for the operator.  Nothing outside the server has a say in it.

  - The account is not this server's at all.  ircu does not register
    accounts, does not keep passwords and does not decide whether a
    credential is good: the node that hosts the network's service bots
    does.  This server records what it is told, sets umode +r, and
    passes it on.  That is the classic Undernet model, and this fork is
    back to it.

The short version of the account half:

  - cli_user()->account is the account name, at most ACCOUNTLEN (12)
    characters, with an optional numeric id (acc_id) and flag word
    (acc_flags) beside it.  IsAccount() tests umode +r.

  - It arrives one of two ways: ACCOUNT (P10 token AC) from a U:lined
    server on a running network, or +r with the name as its parameter
    in the NICK that introduces a user in a burst.

  - There is no -r.  This server cannot take away what it did not
    give.

  - The account survives a nick change: it is not the nickname, and
    nothing about changing one says anything about the other.

  - WHOIS reports it as 330 RPL_WHOISACCOUNT, "<nick> <account> :is
    logged in as".

  - account-notify and extended-join tell a client about it as it
    happens; WHOX %a asks for it.


THE HIDDEN HOST
===============

register_user() calls hide_hostmask() for every user it introduces --
a client of this server or one another server told it about -- before
the user is announced to the network.  The user gets mode +x, and its
visible host (cli_user()->host) becomes

    xxxxxx.yyyyyy.v4        for an IPv4 address
    xxxxxx.yyyyyy.v6        for an IPv6 address

where xxxxxx.yyyyyy are two 32-bit words rendered in the P10 base64
alphabet (A-Z a-z 0-9, never "[" or "]").  The real host stays in
cli_user()->realhost and the real address in cli_ip(); both are what
G-lines match, what operators are shown in WHOIS (338) and WHO %i, and
what the log records (USERIP shows HIDDEN_IP to everyone but the user
itself, operators included, as it always has).  A client without the
chghost capability is told the host in RPL_HOSTHIDDEN (396) right
after the MOTD.

The cipher is TEA, 32 rounds, over the IPv4 address (with a retry
counter in the first input word so that no output ever carries a
bracket) or over the first 64 bits of the IPv6 address (retrying with
the attempt number as initialisation vector).  The key is the
Security block's virtual_host_key, twelve characters of the same
alphabet, split into two 32-bit words.  ircd/ircd_vhost.c is the
implementation, ircd/test/vhost_t.c the vectors it must reproduce,
tests/vhost.py a Python port for the integration tests.  The scheme
and the derivation are those of IRC-Hispano's ircd (crypt/ipvirtual),
so an operator can reproduce a user's host from its address with an
external tool and the same key; only the outputs that tool could not
have used (a bracket in them) differ.

Two users on the same IPv4 address, or on the same IPv6 /64, hide alike;
so the host is still a handle for a ban.  The IPv4 address is spread
over the whole output, not just the second word: the first word carries
half the key and the retry counter, so nothing about the address can
be read off either half without the key.

Because the host is derived rather than carried, every server derives
it.  The NICK that introduces a user on the wire names the real host
and the real address, as it always did; the receiving server hides it
on its own.  This is why the key is a network-wide setting: two servers
with different keys would show the same user under two hosts, and a
ban set on one would not match on the other.

Bots the server introduces itself (+B, and services +S) keep the host
they were configured with; hide_hostmask() leaves them alone and they
do not carry +x.

An account never changes the host.  Upstream ircu rewrote a user's
visible host to <account>.<HIDDEN_HOST> on login; here the visible
host is the cipher of the address and stays that way, so logging in
changes what a user IS called and never what it LOOKS like.  That also
means what a ban or SILENCE mask can match is unchanged: the two
complete identities ~user@realhost and user@hiddenhost, never a mix and
never a third form built out of the account.

The former HOST_HIDING and HIDDEN_HOST features are gone.  HIDDEN_IP
(the address USERIP and WHO %i show for a hidden user, that is, for
every user) and TRUST_USERNAME (drop the ~ from the visible username
of a hidden user, that is, of every user) remain and are described in
readme.features.


THE SECURITY BLOCK
==================

    Security {
            virtual_host_key = "AbCdEfGhIjKl";
    };

The block is mandatory.  A configuration without it, or with a key that
is not exactly twelve characters of A-Z a-z 0-9 [ ], is an error: at
start-up the server reports it and refuses to run; on a rehash it
reports it and keeps the key already in force, since a running server
cannot stop hiding hosts.  A new key on a rehash applies to users who
register afterwards; users already on line keep the host they have.
Nothing shows the key: /STATS does not print it and no numeric carries
it.

The same block, with the same key, goes on every server of the network
(see above).  Generate it once -- twelve random characters of the
alphabet -- and treat it as a secret: with it, anyone can turn a host
back into an address by trying addresses, and the address space is
small.


THE ACCOUNT
===========

An account is a name the network's services keep, and this server's
whole part in it is to remember which one a user showed up with.  It
lives in struct User:

    char     account[ACCOUNTLEN + 1];   /* 12 characters, plus the NUL */
    uint64_t acc_id;                    /* 0 when none was sent */
    uint64_t acc_flags;                 /* 0 when none was sent */

IsAccount() tests umode +r, cli_account() reads the name (and answers
"0" for a user with none, which is what WHOX %a prints).  Everything
that used to key on "has an account" -- channel modes +r and +M, user
mode +R and its sender exemption -- keys on IsAccount() still.

ircu does not manage the lifecycle of any of this.  It does not create
accounts, change passwords, drop accounts or expire them, and it has
no opinion about what an account name may be beyond its length.  All of
that belongs to the node that runs the service bots, which is a
different program; see WHAT THE SERVICES NODE DOES below.

There are two ways an account reaches a server.

1.  ACCOUNT, on a running network
    ------------------------------

        <servernum> AC <target numnick> <account> [<id> [<flags>]]

    ircd/m_account.c.  Only a U:lined server may send it -- a Uworld{}
    block naming the source, checked against the originator and then
    against the uplink, so a multi-hop relay works -- and anything else
    is reported in WALLOPS as a protocol violation and dropped.  The
    server records the name, sets +r, clears +f if it was set (see
    below), tells the client's common channels through account-notify,
    and relays the line on.

    A second ACCOUNT naming a different account for a user who already
    has one is a protocol violation, not a change: the account is not
    something a second opinion settles.  A first-seen id is adopted so
    a later flag update can relay a complete line; an id that
    contradicts one already stored is a violation too.

2.  The NICK burst
    ---------------

        <servernum> N <nick> <hops> <ts> <user> <host> +xr <account> \
                    <b64 ip> <numnick> :<real name>

    +r takes the account as its parameter, and the parameter is
    <account>[:<id>[:<flags>]] -- one token, colon-separated, so a
    burst does not grow a field per number.  ircd/s_user.c reads it in
    do_user_mode() and umode_str() writes it.  That is how a server
    that was not there when the ACCOUNT went past finds out.

And there is no third way.  A MODE +r from a user, on itself or on
anybody else, is put back the way it was before anything is sent: the
gate in do_user_mode() accepts the change only when it arrived on a
server link or a server is its source.  A +S service bot on the
services node reaches it, because its MODE arrives on a link like any
other -- so a bot that just checked a password may log somebody in --
while m_mode.c refuses it over an operator (ERR_USERSDONTMATCH is what
an ordinary user gets for trying at all).  A bot a module created on
this server is neither, so it cannot: what it can do is the rest of
bot_set_user_mode(), and include/bot.h documents that.

There is no -r.  The case in do_user_mode() acts on UMODE_ADD alone, so
a mode string asking to clear it changes nothing and travels nowhere.
Logging a user out is a matter for the services: on this server the
account goes when the user does.

A nick change keeps the account.  It is a separate name for a separate
thing, and a user who changes nick is still whoever the services said
it was.


USER MODE +f, THE FREEZE
========================

+f marks a user that is wearing a nickname it has not shown is its own.
It is this fork's, not upstream's, and it stays: it is the one state
that lets a services package hold somebody still while it works out
whether they may keep the name.

It is set and cleared by a server or a +S service bot, never by the
user -- a user that could take its own +f off could walk out of the one
state that exists to stop it acting under somebody else's name.  Both
directions are restored by the same gate in do_user_mode() that guards
+r.

A frozen user may send only what it needs to stop being frozen:

  AUTHENTICATE                  identify
  PRIVMSG / NOTICE to a +S bot  talk to the service that will lift it
  NICK                          leave the nickname behind
  CAP, PING, PONG, QUIT, ERROR  keep the connection up

Everything else gets ERR_FROZEN (987).  The list is not written in
parse.c: each command declares MFLG_FROZEN_OK in msgtab[], so a command
a module registers can declare it too, and the gate is in
parse_dispatch(), the one place both dispatch paths meet.  WHOIS shows
a frozen user as 692.

Two things lift it:

  - an account arrives.  Both paths do it -- ms_account() and the +r
    case in do_user_mode() -- because an account is exactly the proof
    +f says is missing, and every server applies the same ACCOUNT, so
    every server lets go at the same point with nothing on the wire;
  - a server or a +S bot sends -f.

What happens after that -- a grace period, a rename, a KILL -- is the
services node's policy and not this server's.  ircu holds the state and
enforces what it blocks; it does not decide when to apply it.


WHAT THE SERVICES NODE DOES
===========================

Everything this server does not.  It is a separate program on a link of
its own, U:lined in every server's configuration (Uworld{}), and it is
where the account database, the passwords, the nickname policy and the
service bots live.  From this server's side the whole interface is:

  - ACCOUNT (AC), to say who a user is;
  - MODE, to set +f or -f and whatever else a +S bot may set;
  - XQUERY / XREPLY (doc/readme.xquery), for anything that needs an
    answer -- SASL among them;
  - the ordinary things any server does: KILL, NICK, PRIVMSG.

No SVS* commands are implemented here.  A services package that wants
one adds it through the module API (doc/readme.modules), which is where
a command that is one network's policy belongs.


SASL
====

AUTHENTICATE is relayed, not answered.  ircd/sasl.c and
ircd/m_sasl.c hold one exchange per connection, keyed by a routing
cookie, and everything a client sends goes to the services server as an
XQUERY:

    <ournum> XQ <services numnick> sasl:<cookie> :SASL <...>

and everything it says comes back as an XREPLY with the same routing
prefix, which ircd/m_xreply.c hands back to sasl.c.  The account the
services report is applied by auth_set_account() in ircd/s_auth.c,
which parses <account>[:<id>[:<flags>]] exactly as the burst form does.

Three netconf keys configure it (doc/readme.netconf), which means the
network configures it and not each server:

    sasl.server       the server name to relay to; empty disables SASL
    sasl.mechanisms   what the sasl capability advertises
    sasl.timeout      how long an exchange may take, in seconds

The sasl capability is advertised only while all three conditions hold:
a server name is set, a mechanism list is set, and a server by that
name is actually linked.  A client that negotiated it before the
services split keeps the capability; a new client is not offered it.
/STATS S lists the exchanges in flight.

Numerics 900-908 are the IRCv3 ones and mean what the specification
says.


WHAT A SERVICE BOT MAY DO
=========================

bot_set_user_mode(bot, target, "<modes>") -- include/bot.h.  A +S bot
may change the modes of any user who is not an operator (IsAnOper():
global or local), and is held to what a user may set on itself plus
+r: +o, +O, +k, +S, +B, +z and -x are dropped from the string, the rest
applies.  That rule is enforced on every server the change passes
through, not only the bot's own, so a peer that claims a bot did more
is not believed.  A server itself (a MODE from a server numeric) is not
held to it: it may change anyone's modes to anything, as one server
trusts another about the users it introduces.

A remote +S bot is one a NICK burst introduced with S among its modes;
only a server puts +S on a user, as before.


WHAT A CLIENT SEES
==================

  WHOIS           330 <nick> <account> :is logged in as
                  692 <nick> :is frozen  (this fork's, for +f)
  WHO             %a is the account name, or "0"; %A matches on it
  account-notify  :<nick>!<user>@<host> ACCOUNT <account>, to every
                  client with the capability that shares a channel with
                  the user, the user itself included.  The line carries
                  the name alone -- no id, no flags: those are between
                  servers.
  extended-join   :<nick>!<user>@<host> JOIN <channel> <account>
                  :<real name>, with "*" for a user with no account.
                  A client without the capability gets the plain form;
                  both go out from the same fan-out, so one message is
                  two bodies and nobody sees the other one's.

account-tag is not implemented.  It puts the account on every message a
user sends, which is a tag on every line for something a client can ask
about once.


TESTS
=====

  ircd/test/vhost_t.c           the cipher against IRC-Hispano vectors,
                                key validation, the configuration glue
  ircd/test/user_modes_t.c      the mode letters, +f and +r among them,
                                and that each one keeps its bit
  tests/accounting/             the account from ACCOUNT and from a
                                burst, that a non-U:lined server's is
                                ignored, that there is no -r, that a
                                nick change keeps it, WHOIS 330, WHOX
                                %a, the hidden host on hub and leaf,
                                and who may change whose modes
  tests/account_notify/         who is told about a login and who is
                                not, locally and across a link
  tests/account_flags/          account:id:flags in the NICK burst, and
                                the client ACCOUNT line carrying the
                                name alone
  tests/pr_iauthverify/         a SASL exchange relayed to a fake
                                services server, and what a malformed
                                answer must not do to the ircd
  tests/trust_username/         visible vs. real identity in WHOIS,
                                USERHOST, NAMES, WHO, bans, SILENCE,
                                G-lines
  tests/vhost.py                predicts a client's hidden host
